  "full_response": "A **load balancer** plays a critical role in web application architecture by distributing incoming network traffic across multiple servers to ensure **high availability, scalability, reliability, and performance**. Here’s a breakdown of its key functions:\n\n### **1. Traffic Distribution (Load Balancing)**\n   - Distributes client requests across multiple backend servers (e.g., web servers, application servers, or databases) to prevent any single server from becoming overwhelmed.\n   - Uses algorithms like:\n     - **Round Robin** (sequential distribution)\n     - **Least Connections** (sends traffic to the server with the fewest active connections)\n     - **IP Hash** (consistent routing based on client IP)\n     - **Weighted Distribution** (prioritizes more powerful servers)\n\n### **2. High Availability & Fault Tolerance**\n   - Monitors server health (via **health checks**) and automatically reroutes traffic away from failed or unresponsive servers.\n   - Ensures **zero downtime** by maintaining service even if some servers go offline.\n\n### **3. Scalability**\n   - Allows **horizontal scaling** by adding more servers to handle increased traffic without modifying the application.\n   - Works seamlessly with **auto-scaling groups** (e.g., AWS Auto Scaling, Kubernetes) to dynamically adjust resources.\n\n### **4. Performance Optimization**\n   - Reduces latency by directing users to the nearest or least-loaded server (via **geographic load balancing**).\n   - Offloads tasks like **SSL/TLS termination** (decrypting HTTPS traffic) to improve server efficiency.\n\n### **5. Security Enhancements**\n   - Acts as a **reverse proxy**, hiding backend servers from direct exposure to the internet.\n   - Protects against **DDoS attacks** by filtering malicious traffic before it reaches servers.\n   - Can integrate with **Web Application Firewalls (WAFs)** for additional security.\n\n### **6. Session Persistence (Sticky Sessions)**\n   - Ensures a user’s requests are consistently routed to the same backend server (important for stateful applications like shopping carts).\n   - Uses **cookies** or **IP-based persistence** to maintain session continuity.\n\n### **7. Global Server Load Balancing (GSLB)**\n   - Distributes traffic across multiple **data centers or cloud regions** for disaster recovery and improved user experience.\n   - Directs users to the closest or best-performing data center (e.g., using **DNS-based load balancing**).\n\n### **",
